# Template for the dogfood instance's single declared environment file.
# Install as $FQ_DOGFOOD/.secrets/env (chmod 600). All four launchers —
# run.sh, watcher.sh, dashboard.sh, cron.sh — source it, so it is a POSIX
# shell fragment: command substitution works. run.sh, watcher.sh and
# cron.sh source it with `set -a`, so every assignment reaches their
# process. dashboard.sh reads it the same way but starts the dashboard
# under `env -i` with only the variables the binary reads — FQ_EDGE,
# FQ_EDGE_TOKEN, FQ_EDGE_FINGERPRINT and FQ_DASHBOARD_* — so the one
# web-facing process never holds the provider keys or GH_TOKEN (#545).
#
# This file is the ONLY place the daemon, watcher, dashboard and cron get
# their environment from (#102) — if a variable isn't declared here, the
# process doesn't have it. One file for four processes, so a variable one
# of them reads is set for all of them.

# LLM provider keys — one per provider declared in fqd.toml. A provider
# key missing here fails the daemon at startup (the pricing guarantee),
# not silently at invocation time.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-REPLACE-ME

# GitHub auth for the watcher and for agents' `gh pr create`.
# Deriving it from the current gh login survives token rotation at the
# cost of one ambient lookup; replace with a literal token (or a token
# file refreshed out-of-band) for full hermeticity — open question 1 on
# issue #102.
GH_TOKEN="$(gh auth token)"

# --- Operator dashboard (#105 layer 3) --------------------------------
# The dashboard reads over the daemon's authenticated edge, so it holds
# an identity of its own. All three are required — the binary refuses to
# start without a token or a fingerprint, and dashboard.sh refuses
# without an address.
#
# PORTS FIRST: the dashboard serves on 127.0.0.1:9472, and `[edge] bind`
# DEFAULTS TO THE SAME PORT. One of them has to move. Caddy proxies to
# 9472, so move the edge: set
#
#   [edge]
#   bind = "127.0.0.1:9470"
#
# in **fqd.toml** — the DAEMON's config, host-side next to this file —
# and name the same address here. Not `fq.toml`: that is the client's
# config since the fq/fqd split, it understands `[daemon] addr` and
# nothing else, and an `[edge]` table there is read by nobody. The edit
# looks applied and the edge stays on 9472, straight into the collision
# this paragraph exists to prevent. The daemon reads `fqd.toml` only at
# startup, so restart it after the edit — `fq reload` re-reads the
# agents directory, not the config.
FQ_EDGE=127.0.0.1:9470

# The daemon's certificate fingerprint (SHA-256, hex) — the pin that
# makes the self-signed cert an identity rather than an encryption
# blanket. The daemon printed it when it provisioned its identity (the
# "edge: certificate fingerprint" line at first run) and keeps it in
# <state>/edge/fingerprint; `fq connect` also stores it in
# ~/.config/factor-q/connections.toml. Rotating the daemon's identity
# invalidates it, and every issued token with it.
FQ_EDGE_FINGERPRINT=REPLACE-ME

# The dashboard's capability token — ATTENUATED to the six reads it
# renders, never the admin token. Mint it offline (no daemon round
# trip) from the admin token:
#
#   fq token attenuate --addr "$FQ_EDGE" \
#     --grant read:agent --grant read:control --grant read:cost \
#     --grant read:event --grant read:invocation --grant read:turn
#
# Attenuation only ever narrows, so this token can read exactly the
# domains the pages render and command nothing — a dashboard holding
# the admin token would make that machinery decorative. Re-mint only
# when the daemon's identity is rotated.
FQ_EDGE_TOKEN=REPLACE-ME

# Watcher tuning (defaults shown; uncomment to override). Read by
# watcher.sh and nothing else. Do NOT set FQ_NATS_URL here to steer the
# watcher: that is the daemon's variable (run.sh sources this same file)
# and the watcher no longer reads it — its broker URL is GHW_NATS_URL,
# below.
# FQ_WATCH_REPO=bricef/factor-q
# FQ_WATCH_AGENT=m0-issue-fix
# FQ_WATCH_POLL=60s

# The instance broker's token (#542): one value, three consumers, three
# variables. Generate it with `openssl rand -hex 32`; the broker side is
# `.secrets/nats-auth.conf` holding `authorization { token: "<value>" }`
# (README, "Broker token").
#
#   - The daemon reads it from the variable fqd.toml names under
#     `[nats] token_env` — name FQ_NATS_TOKEN there. It never goes in the
#     daemon's URL: FQ_NATS_URL is printed in the banner, the log and the
#     startup event, and the daemon refuses one that carries a
#     credential (#540).
#   - The Go adapters take it as URL userinfo, each through its own
#     variable. The launchers export the instance default and pass no
#     --nats-url flag, so the token sits in the process environment,
#     never in argv where `ps` would show it.
#
# All three, or none: an adapter left on the token-less default cannot
# connect once the broker requires the token.
FQ_NATS_TOKEN=REPLACE-ME
GHW_NATS_URL=nats://REPLACE-ME@127.0.0.1:4223
FQCRON_NATS_URL=nats://REPLACE-ME@127.0.0.1:4223

# Lifecycle labels the watcher reads/applies. These match the watcher's
# built-in defaults (the status: convention) — set them only to override.
# GHW_READY_LABEL=status:ready
# GHW_IN_PROGRESS_LABEL=status:in-progress
# GHW_IN_REVIEW_LABEL=status:in-review
# GHW_FAILED_LABEL=status:failed
# GHW_DONE_LABEL=status:done
